// === ESP32-S3-EYE blob tracker — class-based, S42-fix version ===
//
// Post-S42 + post-OBJ-IC-POLY ship-ready eye_demo.  Two fixes that
// landed in v0.7.2 are actively exercised here:
//
//   * S42 (OP_SELF + method-flagged OP_CALL): inline nested method
//     calls in argument position work — `List.new(Color.new()
//     .init(...), Color.new().init(...), ...)` correctly wraps each
//     fresh Color instance into the list.  Pre-S42 the outer call
//     silently elided and you got only the last Color.
//
//   * OBJ-IC-POLY (IC fast-path slot-pointer per-recv re-resolve):
//     reading `.r/.g/.b/.tol/.name` from different Color instances
//     of the same shape returns each instance's own values, not the
//     first cached recv's slot.  Pre-fix the IC absolute-pointer
//     cache leaked the first instance's data across all reads.
//
// Reactive runtime exercised:
//   * Two chained at(blob_seen?) handlers (crosshair + counter).
//   * One at(button_pressed?) handler that calls a debounced Cycle
//     method — the C-side ISR fires for every electrical bounce;
//     the script throttles them to one logical press per 200 ms
//     window.  Demonstrates the System.time_us() + class-method
//     pattern for real-world ISR-event handling at the urbiscript
//     level.  (An embedder that wants ISR-level filtering can add
//     it in eye_button.c — we chose script-side here to keep the
//     C scaffolding minimal and showcase the reactive pattern.)
//
// v0.7.x lex/emit guardrails still in effect:
//   * No `[...]` list literal — use List.new(...).
//   * No `&&`/`||` shortcut — nested `if`.
//   * No `%` modulo — wrap table via List.new(1, 2, 0).
//   * Async at-bodies must be a single statement.
//   * Chunk-top `Realm.fn = function() {...}` followed by another
//     Realm SETSLOT corrupts r_global_slot (task #22, separate from
//     S42/S43).  Workaround used here: wrap the debounce + cycle
//     logic in a Cycle class method, instantiate on Realm.

// === Color palette (chrominance-based) ==================================
//
// Switched from absolute-RGB-distance to chrominance 2026-05-16: under
// OV2640 + AWB, GREEN's RGB-distance window matched 5-13% of all pixels
// across the frame even with tight tolerance (diagnostic data: probe G
// = 2500-7400 hits vs RED = 0-716, BLUE = 10-758).  Tightening tol
// either over-matched (tol=12) or zeroed out (tol=8).  RGB-distance is
// fundamentally sensitive to lighting drift.
//
// Chrominance fixes this: a pixel matches when its dominant channel is
// MATERIALLY BRIGHTER than the other two (by `dominance`) AND bright
// enough to not be shadow noise (>= `min_bright`).  Robust against
// AWB shifts because it tests channel SHAPE, not absolute values.
//
// See detect_blob_chroma.h for the C-side detector.
//
// Color slots:
//   dominant   — 0 RED / 1 GREEN / 2 BLUE (which channel must be brightest)
//   dominance  — min margin over the other two channels (5-bit scale, typ 6-12)
//   min_bright — gate noise (5-bit scale, typ 10-16)
class Color {
    var init = function (dominant, dominance, min_bright, name) {
        this.dominant   = dominant;
        this.dominance  = dominance;
        this.min_bright = min_bright;
        this.name       = name;
        this
    }
};

// Per-color dominance tuning (2026-05-16).  Diagnostic data: with
// dominance=4 / min_bright=8, RED scans matched 1000+ pixels per
// held object, BLUE matched 200-500, but GREEN matched only 30-150
// even when the user actively held a green object inside the frame.
// Root cause: the OV2640 AWB actively compensates green saturation
// (AWB tries to neutralize the global colour balance, so a strong
// green object pulls the WHOLE image toward magenta — which then
// re-suppresses the original green's saturation).  Lower GREEN's
// dominance to 2 so weakly-chromatic green pixels still register.
// RED/BLUE keep dominance=4 since they show enough margin without
// AWB fighting back.
Realm.colors = List.new(
    Color.new().init(0, 4, 8, "RED"),
    Color.new().init(1, 2, 6, "GREEN"),
    Color.new().init(2, 4, 8, "BLUE"));

Realm.idx        = 0;
Realm.next_idx   = List.new(1, 2, 0);
Realm.blob_count = 0;

// Telemetry counters for the level-triggered milestone whenever watcher
// (added 2026-05-16 to exercise more reactive surface):
//   Realm.last_milestone keeps the threshold floor — when blob_count grows
//   above last_milestone + 500 the whenever body fires once, bumps
//   last_milestone to blob_count, and the cond becomes false again until
//   blob_count grows another 500.
Realm.last_milestone = 0;
Realm.idx_changes    = 0;
Realm.scan_throws    = 0;
// Bumped from inside the inner try/finally `finally` arm in BlobScan.scan.
// Urbi spec contract (per tests/chk/control_transfer/{try_finally,nested_finally}.chk):
// finally runs ONLY during unwind that PASSES THROUGH the FLAG_HAS_FINALLY
// TRY_FRAME, NOT on normal completion AND NOT when a sibling catch
// absorbs the throw first.  Sequence:
//   - throw fires → walker pops inner TRY_FRAME (FLAG_HAS_FINALLY) →
//     runs finally body → unwind propagates → outer TRY_FRAME absorbs
//   - normal completion path: inner OP_TRY_END pops, JMP past finally
// Demo uses the NESTED pattern (try-finally inside try-catch) to make
// finally actually fire on the unwind path.  Plain try/catch/finally
// would never fire finally because the catch absorbs first.  See
// test_whenever_double_fire.c for both shapes.
// Contract: scan_finallies == scan_throws (every thrown scan unwinds
// through inner finally, then outer catch absorbs).
Realm.scan_finallies = 0;

// === Cycle class — debounced button handler =============================
//
// Empirical: the BOOT button's electrical bounce on the S3-EYE fires
// the IRAM ISR 2-4 times within ~110-150 ms for a single human press.
// 200 ms is comfortably above the bounce envelope yet still allows
// intentional fast tapping at ~5 Hz.
//
// System.time_us() returns the monotonic microsecond counter from
// vm->host_time_us (esp_timer_get_time on ESP-IDF).  press() checks
// the elapsed time since the last accepted fire and either advances
// the cycle or no-ops.
//
// Why a class (vs. `Realm.press = function() {...}`):  chunk-top
// `Realm.fn = function() {...}` followed by any other Realm SETSLOT
// corrupts the global-slot register (task #22).  Wrapping the
// function as a method on a class instance dodges the bug while
// keeping the same call-site shape.
class Cycle {
    var init = function () {
        this.last_us  = 0;
        this.dbnc_us  = 200000;        // 200 ms
        this
    };
    var press = function () {
        var now = System.time_us();
        if (now - this.last_us > this.dbnc_us) {
            this.last_us = now;
            Realm.idx = Realm.next_idx.get(Realm.idx);
            set_target_chroma(
                Realm.colors.get(Realm.idx).dominant,
                Realm.colors.get(Realm.idx).dominance,
                Realm.colors.get(Realm.idx).min_bright);
            log("target =>",
                Realm.colors.get(Realm.idx).name,
                "total blobs:",
                Realm.blob_count)
        }
    }
};

Realm.cycle = Cycle.new().init();

// === Per-watcher fire counters (chained at-handlers below set these) ====
//
// Each at(event?) handler bumps a Realm slot so the Stats snapshot can
// report which handlers actually fired in the last 2s window.  Detects:
//   * Handler starvation (event fires but counter doesn't increment).
//   * Imbalanced fire rates (e.g. blob_seen firing 50× more than expected).
//   * Spurious fires from latent reactive-runtime bugs.
Realm.fires_blob   = 0;
Realm.fires_button = 0;
Realm.fires_stats  = 0;
Realm.fires_scan   = 0;

// === Stats class — every-2s snapshot via diagnostic host fns ============
//
// Pre-stress-test, eye_demo_main.c::port_stats_task formatted the full
// stats line directly via ESP_LOGI.  Stress-test refactor: that task
// now only computes the FPS deltas + injects `stats_tick`.  Each per-
// data-source host fn returns a snapshot; Stats.snapshot() calls all of
// them and emits one big log() line.
//
// Exercises in production (one fire every 2s × N runs):
//   * Cross-task event injection (FreeRTOS stats task -> urbi task SPSC ring).
//   * `at (event?)` body invoking class instance method (task #23 fix).
//   * Class method calling many host fns sequentially without strand growth.
//
// Snapshot fields:
//   * cam=Nfps inject=Nfps              — camera + blob-injection rate
//   * strands=N watchers=N/N             — VM scheduler state
//   * ring=N(max=N) ovf=N                — SPSC event-ring depth + high-water
//   * heap_int=N(min=N)KB psram=N(min=N)KB — heap free + per-interval low-water
//   * steps R=N Q=N W=N F=N              — urbi_step result distribution
//   * fires bl=N btn=N st=N sc=N         — per-watcher fire counts
//   * evt btn=N st=N sc=N                — per-event injection counts
//   * probe R=N G=N B=N                  — per-color C-side blob hits (probe)
//   * center=R/G/B                       — frame-center RGB565 sample
//   * dqbuf_max=Nus                      — DQBUF wait high-water-mark
class Stats {
    var snapshot = function () {
        Realm.fires_stats = Realm.fires_stats + 1;
        log("stats:",
            "cam=",         c_cam_fps(),     "fps inject=", c_inject_fps(), "fps",
            "strands=",     c_strands(),
            "watchers=",    c_watchers_in_use(), "/", c_watchers_high_water(),
            "ring=",        c_ring_max(),
            "ovf=",         c_ring_ovf(),
            "heap_int=",    c_heap_int_kb(), "min=", c_heap_int_min_kb(), "KB",
            "psram=",       c_heap_psram_kb(), "min=", c_heap_psram_min_kb(), "KB",
            "steps R=",     c_step_running(),
            "Q=",           c_step_quiescent(),
            "W=",           c_step_wake_at(),
            "F=",           c_step_fatal(),
            "fires bl=",    Realm.fires_blob,
            "btn=",         Realm.fires_button,
            "st=",          Realm.fires_stats,
            "sc=",          Realm.fires_scan,
            "evt btn=",     c_evt_btn(),
            "st=",          c_evt_stats(),
            "sc=",          c_evt_scan(),
            "probe R=",     c_probe_red(),
            "G=",           c_probe_green(),
            "B=",           c_probe_blue(),
            "center=",      c_center_r(), "/", c_center_g(), "/", c_center_b(),
            "dqbuf_max=",   c_dqbuf_max_wait_us(), "us",
            "ms=",          Realm.last_milestone,
            "idxch=",       Realm.idx_changes,
            "thr=",         Realm.scan_throws,
            "fin=",         Realm.scan_finallies)
    }
};

Realm.stats = Stats.new();

// === Milestone class — body for the level-triggered whenever watcher ====
//
// The whenever watcher (registered at chunk-bottom) fires this every
// time `Realm.blob_count > Realm.last_milestone + 500` becomes true.
// Body sets last_milestone = blob_count so the cond goes false again —
// no infinite-fire loop.  Tests the M5 UWATCHER_WHENEVER level-trigger
// path under real reactive-runtime load.
class Milestone {
    var hit = function () {
        Realm.last_milestone = Realm.blob_count;
        log("MILESTONE: blob_count=", Realm.blob_count, "watchers=", c_watchers_in_use())
    }
};
Realm.milestone = Milestone.new();

// === BlobScan class — full HSV-range scan in urbiscript =================
//
// Every scan_tick (~0.5 Hz, fired by the camera task after memcpy'ing
// the latest frame into the snap buffer), this class loops over the
// 240x240 image at SUBSAMPLE stride and counts pixels matching the
// current Realm.colors[Realm.idx] target.  The C-side detect_blob keeps
// running every frame at 25fps — this stress-test scan runs in parallel
// for pure runtime-load characterisation, not for crosshair feedback.
//
// At subsample=4 the inner loop runs 60x60=3,600 iterations per scan;
// each iteration is 3 host-fn calls (c_get_pixel_r/g/b) + 6 arithmetic
// ops + branch + accumulators.  Expected total: ~10,800 host-fn calls
// and ~70,000 bytecode ops per scan tick.  Crank subsample to 1 for
// the full 240x240 grid (57,600 iterations / 172,800 host-fn calls).
//
// Exercises in production (one fire every 2s × N runs):
//   * Long-running at-body (~0.2s scan time at subsample=4) — pushes
//     the scheduler's cooperative-yield model under sustained load.
//   * Tight nested while loops with arithmetic + branches per iteration.
//   * Per-pixel `this.tr / .tg / .tb` reads — perfect IC fast-path
//     workout (single bytecode site, single recv shape, thousands of
//     reads — pre-OBJ-IC-POLY this would have been silently wrong).
//   * High-rate host-fn dispatch (3 calls per pixel).
//   * Realm slot writes only at end-of-scan (centroid + count).
class BlobScan {
    var init = function (subsample) {
        this.subsample = subsample;
        this.scans = 0;
        this
    };
    var scan = function () {
        // Tell the camera task "I'm scanning — don't queue another
        // scan_tick".  At subsample=1 this body takes seconds; without
        // the guard, scan_ticks at the C-side cadence would pile up in
        // the SPSC event ring.  c_scan_end at the bottom releases.
        c_scan_begin();
        var t0 = System.time_us();
        // Re-target each scan from the current cycle colour.  Lift the
        // chroma fields into locals so the per-pixel hot path doesn't
        // pay for `this.X` reads every iteration.
        var color = Realm.colors.get(Realm.idx);
        var dom_ch     = color.dominant;
        var dominance  = color.dominance;
        var min_bright = color.min_bright;
        var s = this.subsample;

        // Chrominance check: dominant channel must be >= other_two + dominance
        // AND dominant >= min_bright.  Green is 6-bit so we normalize by /2
        // to put all three channels on the same 5-bit scale.
        var hits = 0; var sum_x = 0; var sum_y = 0;
        var y = 0;
        while (y < 240) {
            var x = 0;
            while (x < 240) {
                var r  = c_get_pixel_r(x, y);
                var g  = c_get_pixel_g(x, y) / 2;   // 6-bit -> 5-bit
                var bl = c_get_pixel_b(x, y);
                var dom = 0; var oth1 = 0; var oth2 = 0;
                if (dom_ch == 0) { dom = r;  oth1 = g;  oth2 = bl };
                if (dom_ch == 1) { dom = g;  oth1 = r;  oth2 = bl };
                if (dom_ch == 2) { dom = bl; oth1 = r;  oth2 = g  };
                if (dom >= min_bright) {
                    if (dom - oth1 >= dominance) {
                        if (dom - oth2 >= dominance) {
                            hits = hits + 1;
                            sum_x = sum_x + x;
                            sum_y = sum_y + y
                        }
                    }
                };
                x = x + s
            };
            y = y + s
        };
        this.scans = this.scans + 1;
        var dur_us = System.time_us() - t0;
        // Tier-1d: NESTED try/finally inside try/catch exercising the
        // unwind walker.  Urbi `finally` semantic: runs only when unwind
        // passes through the FLAG_HAS_FINALLY TRY_FRAME.  A plain
        // `try{}catch{}finally{}` would have catch absorb first, leaving
        // finally unreachable — that surprised us at 2026-05-16 (fin=0
        // despite thr>0 in the prior iteration).  The nested form below
        // is the working pattern from nested_finally.chk.
        //   - Even-numbered scans deliberately throw "even-scan-test"
        //   - Zero-hit scans throw "no_match"
        //   - Odd-numbered scans with hits take the normal path
        //   - inner finally runs DURING unwind (before outer catch)
        //   - outer catch absorbs the throw, logs the caught value
        // c_scan_end runs OUTSIDE both try blocks so the camera gate
        // releases on every code path.
        try {
            try {
                // (scans / 2) * 2 == scans → even-number check
                var half = this.scans / 2;
                if (half * 2 == this.scans) {
                    Realm.scan_throws = Realm.scan_throws + 1;
                    throw "even-scan-test"
                };
                if (hits == 0) {
                    Realm.scan_throws = Realm.scan_throws + 1;
                    throw "no_match"
                };
                log("urbi-scan #", this.scans,
                    color.name, "hits=", hits,
                    "centroid=", sum_x / hits, "/", sum_y / hits,
                    "dur_us=", dur_us)
            } finally {
                Realm.scan_finallies = Realm.scan_finallies + 1
            }
        } catch (e) {
            log("urbi-scan #", this.scans, color.name, "caught:", e,
                "dur_us=", dur_us, "throws=", Realm.scan_throws)
        };
        c_scan_end()
    }
};

// subsample=1 → 240x240 grid → 57,600 pixel iterations per scan.
// Empirically ~370 ms/scan at subsample=4 (3,600 iter, log evidence
// 2026-05-16); subsample=1 should be ~16x = ~6 s/scan, which exceeds
// the 2 s scan_tick cadence — the c_scan_begin/c_scan_end guard in
// scan() lets the camera task self-throttle to the actual urbiscript
// throughput.  Lower to 4 / 8 / 16 for less stress.
Realm.scanner = BlobScan.new().init(1);

log("eye_demo (class-based, S42 + OBJ-IC-POLY + scripted stats + urbi-scan) loaded");

// === BLOB SEEN — single at-handler with brace-block body ================
// Stress: collapses 3 separate `at (blob_seen?)` handlers into one body
// using a `{ stmts }` block (canonical urbi form per legacy
// aldebaran-urbi/tests/2.x/at/*.chk — `at (e?) { ... }` is spec-valid).
// Tests register allocation across SEP_SEMI-joined statements within an
// at-event body proto.  Required parser fix S47 (parse_statement_or_expr
// now accepts LBRACE → parse_block).
//
// Previously: 3 separate at-handlers chained — three watcher install
// opcodes, three body protos.  Now: one watcher, one body proto holding
// a brace-block AST_BLOCK.  Side-effect: watcher count drops by 2
// (11 → 9) — visible in Stats `watchers= 9 / 9`.
at (blob_seen?) {
    draw_crosshair(Realm.last_blob_x, Realm.last_blob_y);
    Realm.blob_count = Realm.blob_count + 1;
    Realm.fires_blob = Realm.fires_blob + 1
};

// === BUTTON CYCLE — debounced handler + fire counter ====================
at (button_pressed?) Realm.cycle.press();
at (button_pressed?) Realm.fires_button = Realm.fires_button + 1;

// === STATS TICK — every 2s, snapshot vm/heap counters ===================
// (Stats.snapshot() bumps Realm.fires_stats internally.)
at (stats_tick?) Realm.stats.snapshot();

// === SCAN TICK — every ~2s, full HSV scan in urbiscript ==================
at (scan_tick?) Realm.scanner.scan();
at (scan_tick?) Realm.fires_scan = Realm.fires_scan + 1;

// === Tier-1a: edge-triggered milestone watcher ==========================
// `at (cond) body` — body fires on rising edge of cond (false→true).
// Single fire per crossing.  This is the correct idiom for "alert me
// once per threshold cross" — `whenever` (level-triggered) would fire
// MULTIPLE times because the body's slot-write doesn't propagate before
// the next safepoint visit re-evaluates the cond.  See test_whenever_double_fire.c
// and the design-risks entry "watcher eval-pass requires safepoint visits".
at (Realm.blob_count > Realm.last_milestone + 500) Realm.milestone.hit();

// === Tier-1b: slot-change watcher on Realm.idx ==========================
// Fires every time Realm.idx mutates (cycle press, future auto-cycle).
// Exercises the at(X.changed?) syntax + the slot-change emit + watcher
// dispatch path — different runtime arm than at(event?).
at (Realm.idx.changed?) Realm.idx_changes = Realm.idx_changes + 1;
at (Realm.idx.changed?) log("idx-change #", Realm.idx_changes,
                             "now=", Realm.idx,
                             "color=", Realm.colors.get(Realm.idx).name);
